A dev key, and a door that means zero
=====================================

Two things at the bottom of the rail.

**A key box, labelled "dev key" and nothing else.** Paste an Ollama Cloud key and that is the whole
setup: the key is saved, the provider becomes active, the model is set to `deepseek-v4.1-flash:1017`,
setup is marked answered, and the app opens on a new conversation. No Enter, no button — a key that
has stopped arriving for 600 ms is a key somebody finished typing. It exists because configuring a
build you are testing should cost one paste.

**The door still resets, and now it means zero.** Clearing credentials alone left the conversations
and the memory palace behind, so "back to onboarding" was a rehearsal rather than a reset. The button
clears credentials, unsets the provider and model, deletes the conversations, artifacts and
scratchpad, deletes the memory palace, and marks setup unanswered. The dialog names the palace path
before it goes, because that is the one item somebody might not expect to lose.

The engine refuses to delete a palace outside its own data directory or the home directory the
process started with, and reports what it refused, so the door cannot reach across a filesystem.

The workspace also opens on the memory palace now instead of "No folder selected" — the folder the
assistant is supposed to remember into is a better default than none, and the label reads
`memory-palace` rather than a placeholder.

`quixi-in-container` gives the container a persistent home volume by default (`quixi-home-<name>`),
so saved personas, the palace and the provider list survive a restart; `QUIXI_FRESH=1` asks for the
throwaway profile that every launch rebuilds.

